## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical, beginner-friendly security planning around vendor integrations, token binding, and incident response in a multi-tenant setup.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation
- Audience-bound tokens
- API gateway introspection
- Secrets scanning in CI
- IR playbooks

## Code Example

```javascript
// sample token audience check
function authorize(token, audience, tenantId){
  const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET, { audience });
  if (payload.tenant_id !== tenantId) throw new Error('Tenant mismatch');
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate per-tenant API keys and verify their use in logs?
- What metrics indicate cross-tenant access was prevented?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply practical logging security to prevent token leakage in fintech contexts, a common attack surface.

## Key Concepts
- Log data leakage risks
- Redaction and sanitization
- Middleware for request/response logs
- CI gates for secrets in code
- Incident response and key rotation

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactLog(obj){
  const redacted = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj), (k,v)=>{
    if (/token|secret|api_key|Authorization/i.test(k)) return "[REDACTED]";
    return v;
  });
  return JSON.stringify(redacted);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the redaction effectively?
- How would you handle binary payloads or proprietary log formats?
- What logging libraries and CI tools would you choose and why?
